NNAAMMEE
       groupmod - modify a group definition on the system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ggrroouuppmmoodd [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _G_R_O_U_P

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ggrroouuppmmoodd command modifies the definition of the specified _G_R_O_U_P by
       modifying the appropriate entry in the group database.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The options which apply to the ggrroouuppmmoodd command are:

       --aa, ----aappppeenndd _G_I_D
           If group members are specified with -U, append them to the existing
           member list, rather than replacing it.

       --gg, ----ggiidd _G_I_D
           The group ID of the given _G_R_O_U_P will be changed to _G_I_D.

           The value of _G_I_D must be a non-negative decimal integer. This value
           must be unique, unless the --oo option is used.

           Users who use the group as primary group will be updated to keep
           the group as their primary group.

           Any files that have the old group ID and must continue to belong to
           _G_R_O_U_P, must have their group ID changed manually.

           No checks will be performed with regard to the GGIIDD__MMIINN, GGIIDD__MMAAXX,
           SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMIINN, or SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMAAXX from /etc/login.defs.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help message and exit.

       --nn, ----nneeww--nnaammee _N_E_W___G_R_O_U_P
           The name of the group will be changed from _G_R_O_U_P to _N_E_W___G_R_O_U_P name.

       --oo, ----nnoonn--uunniiqquuee
           When used with the --gg option, allow to change the group _G_I_D to a
           non-unique value.

       --pp, ----ppaasssswwoorrdd _P_A_S_S_W_O_R_D
           The encrypted password, as returned by ccrryypptt(3).

           NNoottee:: This option is not recommended because the password (or
           encrypted password) will be visible by users listing the processes.

           You should make sure the password respects the system's password
           policy.

       --RR, ----rroooott _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R
           Apply changes in the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory and use the configuration
           files from the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory. Only absolute paths are
           supported.

       --PP, ----pprreeffiixx _P_R_E_F_I_X___D_I_R
           Apply changes in the _P_R_E_F_I_X___D_I_R directory and use the configuration
           files from the _P_R_E_F_I_X___D_I_R directory. This option does not chroot
           and is intended for preparing a cross-compilation target. Some
           limitations: NIS and LDAP users/groups are not verified. PAM
           authentication is using the host files. No SELINUX support.

       --UU, ----uusseerrss
           A list of usernames to add as members of the group.

           The default behavior (if the --gg, --NN, and --UU options are not
           specified) is defined by the UUSSEERRGGRROOUUPPSS__EENNAABB variable in
           /etc/login.defs.

CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN
       The following configuration variables in /etc/login.defs change the
       behavior of this tool:

       MMAAXX__MMEEMMBBEERRSS__PPEERR__GGRROOUUPP (number)
           Maximum members per group entry. When the maximum is reached, a new
           group entry (line) is started in /etc/group (with the same name,
           same password, and same GID).

           The default value is 0, meaning that there are no limits in the
           number of members in a group.

           This feature (split group) permits to limit the length of lines in
           the group file. This is useful to make sure that lines for NIS
           groups are not larger than 1024 characters.

           If you need to enforce such limit, you can use 25.

           Note: split groups may not be supported by all tools (even in the
           Shadow toolsuite). You should not use this variable unless you
           really need it.

FFIILLEESS
       /etc/group
           Group account information.

       /etc/gshadow
           Secure group account information.

       /etc/login.defs
           Shadow password suite configuration.

       /etc/passwd
           User account information.

